    "result_text": "All green \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a daily-limit rule to `POST /api/owners` that responds **429** once 100+ owners have been registered today (by `registrationDate`). I mirrored the existing `RejectCrowdedCity` pattern, adding only small cohesive units and touching a single existing file:\n\n**New files (zero surrounding complexity \u2014 each is a fresh, tiny unit):**\n- `RejectDailyLimit.java` \u2014 one function; counts owners whose `registrationDate` is today and throws `DailyLimitException` at \u2265100.\n- `DailyLimitException.java` \u2014 checked exception.\n- `DailyLimitExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.\n- `...escalation.DailyLimitException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**One edited file:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `rejectDailyLimit` step between `rejectCrowdedCity` and `build`.\n\nStructural impact is kept minimal: no existing Java method was modified (so no `WMC_other` cost from any large class), the only edit is a 4-line YAML wiring change, and all new behaviour lives in fresh single-method classes.",
